To optimize the Fibonacci calculation, we can use an iterative approach instead of a recursive one, which reduces the time complexity. Here's an example of an optimized Fibonacci method in C#:

```csharp
public static long Fibonacci(int n)
{
    if (n <= 1)
        return n;

    long previous = 0;
    long current = 1;

    for (int i = 2; i <= n; i++)
    {
        long next = previous + current;
        previous = current;
        current = next;
    }

    return current;
}
```

This method iteratively computes the Fibonacci number, storing only the last two values at any given time, which makes it both time and space efficient.
